 "why": "ERCOT's operating battery fleet went from 87 MW in 2019-01 to 18,204 MW in 2026-08 (EIA-860M). Over the same years the ERCOT hub average's worst-interval multiple fell from 126.6x in 2019 to 12.1x in 2025: the 99.9th percentile went from 2,415 to 312 USD/MWh while the median went from 19.07 to 25.68. Hours at or above USD 1,000 went from 26 in 2019 to 2 in 2025; the worst month was 2019-08 at 465x. With load, Henry Hub and the month of the year held fixed, each added GW of batteries goes with a multiple 10.76 lower (standard error 2.32, p < 0.001). That is an association in a before-and-after with controls, not a cause: the same years brought the 2021 winter storm, a new offer cap, more solar and a gas price that doubled and fell back.",
 "footnote": "Data: ERCOT real-time settlement point prices at HB_HUBAVG, 15-minute intervals, 2019-01 to 2026-10-08 (272,348 intervals in 94 local months, America/Chicago; ERW tables ercot_all_hub_prices_history and iso_rtm_hub_prices from ERCOT NP6-785-ER and NP6-905-CD). Per month and per year: the median and the 99.9th percentile (numpy linear percentiles, no cap or exclusion), their ratio (the worst-interval multiple), hours at or above USD 1,000 and USD 250 (intervals over four), and hours at or above the 99th percentile of every interval in the sample (303.92 USD/MWh, one fixed threshold). Fleet: storage_buildout_monthly, iso:ercot battery_operating_mw (EIA-860M, operating units, month of the data). Load: ercot_zone_load_hourly, the ERCOT total row, monthly mean. Gas: eia_fuel_spot_prices Henry Hub daily spot, monthly mean. Regression: OLS of each monthly spike measure on battery GW, mean load in GW, Henry Hub in USD/MMBtu and eleven month-of-year dummies, 92 months 2019-01 to 2026-08 (months after the latest fleet figure are left out), HC1 standard errors, p from Student's t. No instrument, no event window: an association.",
